**What and Why:**
ORM = Object Relational Mapper. Instead of writing raw SQL queries, you write Python to talk to your database. Django converts your Python into SQL automatically. This is how your entire AI Job Portal and SaaS Expense Manager will talk to the database — no raw SQL needed. Master this today — you'll use it every single day in both projects.

---

```python
# ============================================================
# CONCEPT 1: ORM BASICS — OPEN DJANGO SHELL
# ============================================================

# Open terminal, activate venv, run:
# python manage.py shell

# Import your model first — always:
from blog.models import Post

# ============================================================
# CONCEPT 2: CREATE — ADD DATA TO DATABASE
# ============================================================

# WAY 1 — create() — creates AND saves in one step
p1 = Post.objects.create(
    title="My First Post",
    content="Hello Django ORM",
    is_published=True
)
# Post.objects = Django's manager — gateway to database operations
# .create() = INSERT INTO blog_post (...) VALUES (...) in SQL
# p1 = Post object with id=1, created_at set automatically
print(p1.id)        # 1
print(p1.title)     # My First Post
print(p1.created_at) # 2024-01-15 10:30:00

# WAY 2 — create object then save separately
p2 = Post(title="Second Post", content="Learning ORM")
# creates object in memory — NOT saved to database yet
p2.is_published = True  # can set attributes before saving
p2.save()
# save() = now INSERT INTO database
# use this when you need to set/modify attributes before saving

# WAY 3 — get_or_create() — creates only if doesn't exist
p3, created = Post.objects.get_or_create(
    title="Third Post",
    defaults={"content": "ORM is powerful"}
)
# created = True if new object created
# created = False if existing object found
# useful to avoid duplicate entries
print(created)  # True first time, False if run again
```

